1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,266
Release day.

2
00:00:01,266 --> 00:00:02,350
The app is ready.

3
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:04,902
Then the security team finds a password,

4
00:00:04,902 --> 00:00:06,306
written in plain text,

5
00:00:06,306 --> 00:00:07,327
inside the code.

6
00:00:07,877 --> 00:00:09,032
The release stops.

7
00:00:09,032 --> 00:00:10,315
Three weeks of work,

8
00:00:10,315 --> 00:00:10,828
waiting.

9
00:00:10,828 --> 00:00:11,983
And the same week,

10
00:00:11,983 --> 00:00:13,202
the site goes down,

11
00:00:13,202 --> 00:00:16,474
and nobody can say how much downtime is acceptable.

12
00:00:17,024 --> 00:00:18,280
Good teams fix both.

13
00:00:18,280 --> 00:00:20,793
They check security inside the pipeline,

14
00:00:20,793 --> 00:00:23,243
they measure reliability with a number,

15
00:00:23,243 --> 00:00:26,133
and they give everyone a paved road to follow.

16
00:00:26,683 --> 00:00:27,976
Today: DevSecOps,

17
00:00:27,976 --> 00:00:28,384
SRE,

18
00:00:28,384 --> 00:00:30,085
and platform engineering.

19
00:00:30,085 --> 00:00:31,377
Three modern ideas,

20
00:00:31,377 --> 00:00:33,894
and what each one means for your job.

21
00:00:35,444 --> 00:00:36,873
Here is what to expect.

22
00:00:37,423 --> 00:00:40,773
One: DevSecOps, and why security moves to the left.

23
00:00:41,323 --> 00:00:44,928
Two: SRE, and a clever idea called the error budget.

24
00:00:45,478 --> 00:00:49,382
Three: platform engineering, and how teams share one paved road.

25
00:00:49,932 --> 00:00:51,108
Four: a live demo.

26
00:00:51,108 --> 00:00:53,265
A security gate catches a secret,

27
00:00:53,265 --> 00:00:55,422
and we calculate an error budget,

28
00:00:55,422 --> 00:00:57,121
from real request numbers.

29
00:00:57,671 --> 00:01:01,085
This is lesson five of the DevOps fundamentals domain.

30
00:01:03,135 --> 00:01:04,607
Start with DevSecOps.

31
00:01:05,157 --> 00:01:06,089
In the old way,

32
00:01:06,089 --> 00:01:07,270
security came last.

33
00:01:07,270 --> 00:01:10,067
A separate team checked the finished product,

34
00:01:10,067 --> 00:01:11,310
just before release.

35
00:01:11,310 --> 00:01:14,480
Problems found there are slow and expensive to fix.

36
00:01:15,030 --> 00:01:17,704
DevSecOps moves security to the left.

37
00:01:17,704 --> 00:01:18,253
Earlier.

38
00:01:18,253 --> 00:01:19,213
Into the code,

39
00:01:19,213 --> 00:01:20,721
and into the pipeline.

40
00:01:20,721 --> 00:01:22,710
Engineers call it shift left.

41
00:01:23,260 --> 00:01:25,573
So the pipeline gets security steps.

42
00:01:25,573 --> 00:01:27,245
Scan the code for secrets.

43
00:01:27,245 --> 00:01:29,494
Scan the libraries for known holes.

44
00:01:29,494 --> 00:01:31,101
Scan the container image.

45
00:01:31,101 --> 00:01:33,350
And check the infrastructure files.

46
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:35,224
Each scan is a gate.

47
00:01:35,224 --> 00:01:36,018
If it fails,

48
00:01:36,018 --> 00:01:37,871
the change does not move on,

49
00:01:37,871 --> 00:01:39,724
exactly like a failing test.

50
00:01:40,274 --> 00:01:42,910
And security stops being one team's job.

51
00:01:42,910 --> 00:01:44,623
It becomes everyone's job,

52
00:01:44,623 --> 00:01:46,205
with the tools built in.

53
00:01:47,755 --> 00:01:50,976
Next, SRE. Site reliability engineering.

54
00:01:51,526 --> 00:01:52,909
It started at Google.

55
00:01:52,909 --> 00:01:56,792
The idea: run operations the way a software engineer would.

56
00:01:56,792 --> 00:01:57,450
With code,

57
00:01:57,450 --> 00:01:58,109
with data,

58
00:01:58,109 --> 00:01:59,228
and with numbers.

59
00:01:59,778 --> 00:02:01,708
So reliability gets a number.

60
00:02:01,708 --> 00:02:02,107
First,

61
00:02:02,107 --> 00:02:02,707
an SLI,

62
00:02:02,707 --> 00:02:05,968
a service level indicator: something you measure,

63
00:02:05,968 --> 00:02:08,631
like the share of requests that succeed.

64
00:02:09,181 --> 00:02:10,150
Then an SLO,

65
00:02:10,150 --> 00:02:12,779
a service level objective: the target.

66
00:02:12,779 --> 00:02:13,609
For example,

67
00:02:13,609 --> 00:02:17,138
99.9 percent of requests succeed.

68
00:02:17,688 --> 00:02:19,174
An SLA is different.

69
00:02:19,174 --> 00:02:21,200
It is a promise to a customer,

70
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:22,955
often with money attached.

71
00:02:22,955 --> 00:02:25,049
The SLO is the internal goal,

72
00:02:25,049 --> 00:02:26,264
a little stricter.

73
00:02:26,814 --> 00:02:30,772
And here is a surprise: the target is never one hundred percent.

74
00:02:30,772 --> 00:02:33,369
A hundred percent is impossibly expensive,

75
00:02:33,369 --> 00:02:35,966
and users would not notice the difference.

76
00:02:37,516 --> 00:02:39,692
That gap has a name: the error budget.

77
00:02:40,242 --> 00:02:43,122
If the target is 99.9 percent,

78
00:02:43,122 --> 00:02:46,122
you are allowed to fail one request in a thousand.

79
00:02:46,122 --> 00:02:47,922
That allowance is your budget.

80
00:02:48,472 --> 00:02:52,440
Think of it as a monthly allowance. Every failure spends a little of it.

81
00:02:52,990 --> 00:02:56,510
Budget left? Ship new features, take risks. It is healthy.

82
00:02:57,060 --> 00:03:00,345
Budget almost gone? Ship small, careful changes.

83
00:03:00,895 --> 00:03:04,714
Budget spent? Freeze features, and fix reliability first.

84
00:03:05,264 --> 00:03:07,079
This ends the old argument.

85
00:03:07,079 --> 00:03:08,558
Developers want speed,

86
00:03:08,558 --> 00:03:10,306
operations want stability.

87
00:03:10,306 --> 00:03:14,608
The budget is the shared rule that both teams agreed in advance.

88
00:03:16,158 --> 00:03:18,313
Third idea: platform engineering.

89
00:03:18,863 --> 00:03:20,141
As a company grows,

90
00:03:20,141 --> 00:03:22,967
every team solves the same problems again.

91
00:03:22,967 --> 00:03:24,918
Each builds its own pipeline,

92
00:03:24,918 --> 00:03:25,995
its own servers,

93
00:03:25,995 --> 00:03:27,609
its own security checks.

94
00:03:28,159 --> 00:03:30,876
A platform team builds those things once,

95
00:03:30,876 --> 00:03:31,738
as a product.

96
00:03:31,738 --> 00:03:32,599
A paved road.

97
00:03:32,599 --> 00:03:34,587
Teams get a standard pipeline,

98
00:03:34,587 --> 00:03:36,045
standard environments,

99
00:03:36,045 --> 00:03:37,503
and security built in.

100
00:03:38,053 --> 00:03:39,832
Developers serve themselves.

101
00:03:39,832 --> 00:03:40,531
No tickets,

102
00:03:40,531 --> 00:03:41,230
no waiting.

103
00:03:41,230 --> 00:03:42,819
The road is the easy way,

104
00:03:42,819 --> 00:03:43,963
so they choose it.

105
00:03:44,513 --> 00:03:47,690
The platform team treats developers as its customers,

106
00:03:47,690 --> 00:03:50,507
and measures success by how smoothly they ship.

107
00:03:52,057 --> 00:03:53,593
How do the three fit together?

108
00:03:54,143 --> 00:03:56,969
Platform engineering builds the paved road.

109
00:03:56,969 --> 00:04:00,188
DevSecOps puts the security gates on that road.

110
00:04:00,188 --> 00:04:03,343
And SRE measures whether the road is reliable,

111
00:04:03,343 --> 00:04:04,788
with the error budget.

112
00:04:05,338 --> 00:04:09,157
Different jobs, one goal: ship fast, safely, and reliably.

113
00:04:09,707 --> 00:04:13,168
Notice the pattern from the whole domain: shared ownership,

114
00:04:13,168 --> 00:04:13,990
fast feedback,

115
00:04:13,990 --> 00:04:14,635
automation,

116
00:04:14,635 --> 00:04:15,574
and measurement.

117
00:04:16,124 --> 00:04:17,259
In the next lesson,

118
00:04:17,259 --> 00:04:19,469
we take the measurement idea further,

119
00:04:19,469 --> 00:04:21,500
with the four key DevOps metrics.

120
00:04:23,050 --> 00:04:27,295
Go to the demo folder.

121
00:04:28,845 --> 00:04:30,744
Look around.

122
00:04:31,294 --> 00:04:36,521
Source code, request data, and two scripts: a security scan, and a budget calculator.

123
00:04:37,071 --> 00:04:42,639
The request data.

124
00:04:43,189 --> 00:04:46,069
Seven days: total requests, and how many failed.

125
00:04:46,619 --> 00:04:50,565
Run the security gate.

126
00:04:51,115 --> 00:04:51,427
Fail.

127
00:04:51,427 --> 00:04:53,793
A password is written inside the code.

128
00:04:53,793 --> 00:04:55,537
This change would stop here,

129
00:04:55,537 --> 00:04:57,281
before any customer sees it.

130
00:04:57,831 --> 00:05:02,865
Fix it.

131
00:05:03,415 --> 00:05:04,333
Scan again.

132
00:05:05,883 --> 00:05:07,760
Pass. No secrets in the code.

133
00:05:08,310 --> 00:05:16,737
Budget, at 99.9.

134
00:05:17,287 --> 00:05:21,939
About five hundred thirty failures against seven hundred thousand requests.

135
00:05:21,939 --> 00:05:25,103
The budget allows six hundred ninety-nine failures,

136
00:05:25,103 --> 00:05:27,088
and we used 47 percent.

137
00:05:27,088 --> 00:05:27,399
Ship.

138
00:05:27,949 --> 00:05:31,127
A stricter target.

139
00:05:31,677 --> 00:05:36,051
Same failures, but a smaller budget. Ninety-four percent used. Careful.

140
00:05:36,601 --> 00:05:38,350
Stricter again.

141
00:05:39,700 --> 00:05:44,073
Freeze. The budget is spent. The stricter the promise, the less room to fail.

142
00:05:44,623 --> 00:05:48,463
Write it down.

143
00:05:58,713 --> 00:06:01,231
Check the file.

144
00:06:02,781 --> 00:06:06,024
Your practice task: catch a secret, and spend a budget.

145
00:06:06,574 --> 00:06:08,771
One: run the scan, and see the failure.

146
00:06:09,321 --> 00:06:12,436
Two: fix the password, and scan again. It must pass.

147
00:06:12,986 --> 00:06:16,634
Three: run the budget at three targets, and compare the answers.

148
00:06:17,184 --> 00:06:18,762
Four: write your three lines.

149
00:06:20,812 --> 00:06:24,033
Where to find the demo files. Pause here, and write it down.

150
00:06:24,583 --> 00:06:30,258
One: the repository, github.com/it-waves/devops-ai.

151
00:06:30,808 --> 00:06:34,989
Two: the folder academy/lesson149-demo.

152
00:06:35,539 --> 00:06:36,930
Three: a source folder,

153
00:06:36,930 --> 00:06:37,776
a data folder,

154
00:06:37,776 --> 00:06:38,501
two scripts,

155
00:06:38,501 --> 00:06:39,468
scan and budget,

156
00:06:39,468 --> 00:06:41,342
and a read me with the answers.

157
00:06:41,892 --> 00:06:43,597
Four: clone the repository,

158
00:06:43,597 --> 00:06:44,861
and copy the folder.

159
00:06:44,861 --> 00:06:45,934
You need a shell,

160
00:06:45,934 --> 00:06:46,440
and awk,

161
00:06:46,440 --> 00:06:48,335
which every Linux machine has.

162
00:06:48,885 --> 00:06:49,620
Five: again,

163
00:06:49,620 --> 00:06:50,969
no lab in this domain.

164
00:06:50,969 --> 00:06:52,318
The practice is plain,

165
00:06:52,318 --> 00:06:54,709
and the answers are on the next screen.

166
00:06:56,759 --> 00:06:59,234
Check yourself. Six points. Tick each one.

167
00:06:59,784 --> 00:07:02,514
One: the scan fails on the hard-coded password.

168
00:07:03,064 --> 00:07:05,262
Two: after the fix, the scan passes.

169
00:07:05,812 --> 00:07:09,118
Three: at 99.9, the budget is healthy.

170
00:07:09,668 --> 00:07:12,356
Four: at 99.95, careful.

171
00:07:12,906 --> 00:07:15,637
Five: at 99.99, freeze.

172
00:07:16,187 --> 00:07:19,067
Six: your file has three lines, in your own words.

173
00:07:19,617 --> 00:07:22,092
All six, and you understand the three ideas.

174
00:07:24,142 --> 00:07:25,913
Your turn, on your own machine.

175
00:07:26,463 --> 00:07:28,297
One: copy the demo folder.

176
00:07:28,847 --> 00:07:31,962
Two: run the scan, fix the password, and scan again.

177
00:07:32,512 --> 00:07:36,139
Three: run the budget three times, with three different targets.

178
00:07:36,689 --> 00:07:38,267
Four: write your three lines.

179
00:07:38,817 --> 00:07:43,767
Five: tick the six points. Then think about your own service: what would its target be?

180
00:07:44,317 --> 00:07:46,151
Pause the video, and begin now.

181
00:08:01,701 --> 00:08:02,576
Quick check.

182
00:08:03,126 --> 00:08:04,555
What does shift left mean?

183
00:08:05,105 --> 00:08:06,492
What is an error budget?

184
00:08:07,042 --> 00:08:08,919
What happens when the budget is spent?

185
00:08:09,469 --> 00:08:11,112
What does a platform team build?

186
00:08:11,662 --> 00:08:14,079
Objective covered: MC16,

187
00:08:14,079 --> 00:08:16,263
objective five: DevSecOps,

188
00:08:16,263 --> 00:08:16,731
SRE,

189
00:08:16,731 --> 00:08:18,680
and platform engineering.

190
00:08:19,230 --> 00:08:21,300
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

191
00:08:31,850 --> 00:08:34,623
Next time: the four key DevOps metrics.

